            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Heliocentric Theory</strong><br>\nCopernicus is best known for proposing the heliocentric theory, which suggests that the sun is at the center of the universe and that the Earth and other planets orbit around it. This was a revolutionary idea at the time, as the prevailing belief was the geocentric model, which placed the Earth at the center. Copernicus's theory challenged the way people understood their place in the cosmos.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Commentariolus\" - The Little Commentary</strong><br>\nAround 1514, Copernicus wrote a short treatise known as \"Commentariolus.\" In it, he outlined his heliocentric theory, describing the Earth's motion in three ways: rotation, revolution, and annual tilting of the Earth's axis. This manuscript was not formally published but circulated among his friends and scholars, gradually gaining attention for its daring ideas.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"De revolutionibus orbium coelestium\"</strong><br>\nCopernicus's major work, \"On the Revolutions of the Celestial Spheres,\" was completed in 1532 but published just before his death in 1543. This book provided a comprehensive mathematical explanation for the heliocentric theory and marked a significant shift in the field of astronomy, laying the groundwork for modern astronomy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
