          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Copernicus's Later Years</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Copernicus continued his clerical duties and his observations of the planets and stars. He lived through the early Reformation period, which saw Europe beginning to question long-standing beliefs and practices. Copernicus's own work reflected this age of inquiry, as it questioned and ultimately changed the established understanding of the universe.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Impact</strong><br>\nAfter his death, Copernicus's ideas faced both support and controversy, particularly from the Catholic Church, which later placed his book on the index of prohibited books. However, his model influenced other scientists like Galileo Galilei and Johannes Kepler, who built upon his theory, and contributed to the Scientific Revolution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Copernicus's Death</strong><br>\nNicolaus Copernicus died on May 24, 1543, in Frombork, Poland. His passing was hardly noted at the time, but his legacy endured, eventually altering the course of science. He was laid to rest in the same cathedral where he had served as a canon, but his exact burial place remained unknown until his remains were identified in 2005 through DNA analysis.</p>\n"
              },
